$\triangle ABC$ and $\triangle DBC$ share $BC$. $AB = 5\ \text{cm}$, $AC = 12\ \text{cm}$, $DC = 8\ \text{cm}$, and $BD = 20\ \text{cm}$. What is the least possible integral number of centimeters in $BC$?

[asy]
size(100); import graph; currentpen = fontsize(10pt);
pair B = (0,0), C = (13,0), A = (-5,7), D = (16,10);

draw(B--A--C--cycle); draw(B--D--C);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,E);
[/asy]

solution:
The area of triangle $ABC$ is given by
\[[ABC] = \frac{1}{2} AC \cdot BC \cdot \sin C.\][asy]
unitsize (1 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = (0,0);
B = (5,0);
C = (1,2);
D = extension(C, incenter(A,B,C), A, B);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(C--D);

label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$D$", D, S);
label("$6$", (C + D)/2, NE);
[/asy]

We can also write
\begin{align*}
[ABC] &= [ACD] + [BCD] \\
&= \frac{1}{2} AC \cdot CD \sin \frac{C}{2} + \frac{1}{2} BC \cdot CD \sin \frac{C}{2} \\
&= 3AC \sin \frac{C}{2} + 3BC \sin \frac{C}{2} \\
&= 3 (AC + BC) \sin \frac{C}{2}.
\end{align*}Thus,
\[\frac{1}{2} AC \cdot BC \cdot \sin C = 3(AC + BC) \sin \frac{C}{2}.\]Then
\[AC \cdot BC \sin \frac{C}{2} \cos \frac{C}{2} = 3(AC + BC) \sin \frac{C}{2},\]so
\[\frac{AC \cdot BC}{3} = 3 (AC + BC).\]Hence,
\[\frac{1}{AC} + \frac{1}{BC} = \frac{AC + BC}{AC \cdot BC} = \boxed{\frac{1}{9}}.\]

